      "side": 1,
      "message": "Awesome!\nOne thing I\u0027m not sure about--some places use sk_sp\u003c\u003e, others don\u0027t. But I couldn\u0027t tell if ownership is actually changing hands anywhere here. If not, sk_sp seems like it might not be necessary. Also, I wasn\u0027t entirely sure if a null SkBlender was meant to be valid or not.",
      "revId": "48a2ea10279fb5008176cc21cb572b5098c91100",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0651af7a_f8bcc081",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5085
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-11-16T15:17:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s mostly not supposed to be null. The exception is the sk_paint_to_grpaint_impl where null indicates we\u0027re not in a scenario where there is a primitive color to blend against the shader as that\u0027s a common implementation function for all the SkPaintToGrPaint* functions, including ones that don\u0027t take a blender.\n\nWe\u0027re not very consistent about passing SkFoo* vs sk_sp\u003cSkFoo\u003e, esp at the public API level (esp. where SkFoo is SkImage). I noticed one of the newer APIs, clipShader, only takes sk_sp\u003cSkShader\u003e at the SkCanvas level and then below in the implementation. That seemed like a nice model. I don\u0027t think we see much cost for additional ref\u0027ing, esp when we use move when possible internally. It seemed like the most defensive choice, so I followed that here, with the exception of Sk-\u003eGr paint conversion, but maybe that should also just take sk_sp for consistency. I\u0027m not strongly attached to any of this.",
      "parentUuid": "03c7ef3c_15b0ec35",
      "revId": "48a2ea10279fb5008176cc21cb572b5098c91100",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "953f63fa_43e9c544",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-11-16T18:03:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "sk_sp enforces a requirement on the caller that a raw pointer does not (you must have a sk_sp\u003c\u003e to pass one) so IMO we shouldn\u0027t require it unless ownership matters in some way. It\u0027s not a big deal if we consistently use sk_sp *everywhere* since then, of course you will have it, but that\u0027s got its own cost (paying for refcounting everywhere even when you don\u0027t need it). So I don\u0027t feel *strongly* about it, but I have been boxed in in the past by inconsistent use of shared-ptrs so I do have opinions here :) I feel like it\u0027s better to pass raw pointers if a raw pointer is all you actually care about, and limit the scope of ownership concerns where it\u0027s safe to do so.\n\nThe \"null\" question was to narrow in on, should this be a `const SkBlender*`, or a `const SkBlender\u0026`?",
      "parentUuid": "0651af7a_f8bcc081",
      "revId": "48a2ea10279fb5008176cc21cb572b5098c91100",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "208ea3dd_7d035e1a",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5085
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-11-16T22:46:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So at the Canvas level, if we expose SkBlender there, we\u0027ll definitely want sk_sp since for the recording canvases. \n\nWithin Device implementations we sometimes pass the blender into wrapper classes that expect sk_sp. (In those cases the wrapper only lasts for the call stack, but they\u0027re subclasses of types like SkShader that are always ref counted). Sk-\u003eGr paint seems unlikely to ever want to retain.\n\nSo maybe what we have here is fine.\n\nA difference between sk_sp and shared_ptr is that sk_sp works with objects that have internal ref counts. So, really, you can always make a sk_sp from a bare pointer. There\u0027s only ever one ref count, internal to the object. (Of course the downside is reference counting is built into the class rather than layered on later at each use case so if you somehow put an object on the stack it could still be reffed/unreffed. We generally prevent that by enforcing factory functions for creation. Sometimes I wish we had the composability of shared_ptr).",
      "parentUuid": "953f63fa_43e9c544",
      "revId": "48a2ea10279fb5008176cc21cb572b5098c91100",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
